Third-party components in phone-ai-kiosk
========================================

openWakeWord — wake word detection
----------------------------------
The three ONNX files under app/src/main/assets/models/ come from openWakeWord,
by David Scripka: https://github.com/dscripka/openWakeWord

    melspectrogram.onnx        release v0.5.1
    embedding_model.onnx       release v0.5.1
    hey_jarvis_v0.1.onnx       release v0.5.1

THESE MODELS ARE NOT APACHE-LICENSED. openWakeWord's own README draws the line
in its License section, quoted here in full so nobody has to go and look:

    All of the code in this repository is licensed under the Apache 2.0
    license. All of the included pre-trained models are licensed under the
    Creative Commons Attribution-NonCommercial-ShareAlike 4.0 International
    license due to the inclusion of datasets with unknown or restrictive
    licensing as part of the training data. If you are interested in
    pre-trained models with more permissive licensing, please raise an issue
    and we will try to add them to a future release.

So: the code is Apache-2.0, the models are CC BY-NC-SA 4.0
(https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-sa/4.0/), and the models are what
this repository redistributes.

WHAT THAT MEANS HERE. This project is a personal home kiosk and is not
commercial, which is what NonCommercial asks. ShareAlike is why the repository
as a whole is under CC BY-NC-SA 4.0 rather than a permissive licence: the models
are shipped inside the APK, so the simplest way to be certainly compliant is for
the whole thing to carry the same terms. See LICENSE.

If this project is ever sold, put behind a paywall, or used in a business, these
model files must come out first. That is not a nice-to-have reading of the
licence; NonCommercial is the term the models are offered under.

The Kotlin wake word pipeline in app/src/main/java/.../voice/WakeWord.kt is a
re-implementation of openWakeWord's streaming feature extraction, written from
its Apache-2.0 source (utils.py, model.py) and held to its numbers by
app/src/test/java/.../WakeWordParityTest.kt.

Google speech_embedding
-----------------------
embedding_model.onnx is openWakeWord's re-implementation of Google's
speech_embedding model (https://tfhub.dev/google/speech_embedding/1, described
in https://arxiv.org/abs/2002.01322), which Google publishes under Apache-2.0.
openWakeWord's README states that upstream licence for the original TFHub
module; the weights redistributed here arrive through openWakeWord, whose
README places all its included pre-trained models under CC BY-NC-SA 4.0. Where
the two readings differ, this repository follows the stricter one.

Press Start 2P — the pixel face
-------------------------------
app/src/main/res/font/press_start_2p.ttf is Press Start 2P by CodeMan38,
taken from Google Fonts (https://fonts.google.com/specimen/Press+Start+2P,
source of the exact file: https://github.com/google/fonts, ofl/pressstart2p).

    Copyright 2012 The Press Start 2P Project Authors (cody@zone38.net),
    with Reserved Font Name "Press Start 2P".

Licensed under the SIL Open Font License, Version 1.1. The full text as
shipped with the font is at licenses/fonts/PressStart2P-OFL.txt.

The OFL permits bundling the font in an application, including a commercial
one, and asks three things of anyone doing it: do not sell the font on its
own, keep this notice with it, and do not release a modified version under
the Reserved Font Name. The file here is unmodified, which is the simplest
way to hold to the third.

It carries no Thai. That is deliberate and is why ui/RetroType.kt exists: the
Latin and the digits are set in this face and the Thai in the one below, with
the sizes and the baseline reconciled in code rather than left to whatever
font the device would have fallen back to.

